+     "description": "The ANSWER over the examined partition, and the second committed encoding of the section 4.1 claim-1 question `did we look at every subject?`. RC-6, enforced by the cross-property admission check rather than encoded in this shape schema: coverage=complete REQUIRES resolutionCompleteness.examinedExhaustive=true. It is an IMPLICATION, never an equality. coverage=unknown constrains examinedExhaustive in NEITHER direction: a host may examine the committed partition exhaustively and still answer unknown because the evidence it needs is missing rather than unexamined (ambiguous or missing Rust compilation ownership, an undeclared or unservable capability, an unlisted source variant), and it may also answer unknown because the examination itself was cut short. Only the converse is impossible: complete over a partition the producer says it did not examine exhaustively asserts a totality the same record disclaims. RC-6 says nothing about resolutionCompleteness.state - enumeration completeness and RESOLUTION completeness are different claims, and RC-3's coverage=complete with state=incomplete over real unresolved edges stays lawful. It is total over every registered (relation, rung) pair including the twelve non-resolved ones, holds for a fact-free entry over an empty examined scope, and is enforced by coverage_bijection at the producer boundary (admit_coverage_result_v3) AND again at retained Run closure, whose disagreement is PROVIDER.PROTOCOL_VIOLATION on the existing native.coverage-bijection-mismatch carrier - no new key and no new public detail."
